# Purpose
Implement production-grade REST consumption with robust errors/offline behavior, safe logging/secrets, and correct EAS Build vs Update discipline.

## Do / Don't

### REST API (DO)
DO centralize all HTTP in a single client module (path must match repo; do not guess).
DO enforce HTTPS base URL via environment/config (no hardcoded secrets).
DO define a typed error model and map to user-friendly UI states:
  - kind: network | http | parse
  - status (when http), message, retriable flag
DO set explicit timeouts and retries:
  - Retry only idempotent requests by default (GET/HEAD).
  - Never retry unsafe mutations by default (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) unless explicitly designed to be idempotent.
DO provide offline/failed-network UX:
  - clear message + retry action
  - optional caching only with stated invalidation rules

### Security/Privacy (DO)
DO keep secrets out of source control:
  - Use .env (ignored) and/or EAS secrets; centralize env access in one module.
DO redact logs:
  - Authorization headers, tokens, cookies, and PII (emails/phones/addresses/payment info).
DO avoid showing raw server error payloads to users.

### EAS Update / Build (DO)
DO configure updates using EAS tooling when needed:
  - eas update:configure
DO treat runtimeVersion as the OTA compatibility boundary.
DO require an EAS build when:
  - adding/updating native deps
  - changing native config/plugins
  - changing runtimeVersion strategy

### DON'T
MUST NOT scatter fetch usage across UI components.
MUST NOT log secrets/PII.
MUST NOT ship an OTA update that depends on native changes not in the installed binary.

## Commands (npm for local scripts; EAS via CLI)
Local:
  - npx expo doctor
  - npm run start (if present) else npx expo start
EAS:
  - eas update:configure
  - eas build --platform ios --profile production
  - eas build --platform android --profile production
  - eas update --branch production --message "..."

## Completion Checklist
[ ] REST calls centralized behind one client module.
[ ] Typed error model implemented and surfaced as UX states.
[ ] Retry/timeout behavior explicit and safe.
[ ] Offline/failed-network behavior handled for affected flows.
[ ] No secrets/PII logged or committed.
[ ] OTA-safe vs requires rebuild correctly classified (runtimeVersion/native impact).

## Common Pitfalls
Retrying non-idempotent requests causing duplicate actions.
Debug logging request headers including Authorization.
OTA update published after native dependency/config change (crash on user devices).
"Caching" without invalidation leading to stale/broken UI.
